[abstract]
The Jacobian Conjecture has remained open since 1939 despite decades of work in
algebraic geometry and complex analysis. This paper proposes an alternative algebraic
framework based on Jordan algebras, fixed-point operators, and quantum-inspired
linear algebraic constructions.

The central construction introduces a Jordan operator:

    T(ρ) = φ⁻¹UρU† + φ⁻²ρ

where φ is the golden ratio and U is the unitary evolution generated from a polynomial
Hamiltonian associated with a Keller map. The fixed point satisfies:

    [U, ρ*] = 0

placing it inside the commutant of the associated unitary — a path to polynomial
invertibility without complex-analytic machinery.

[core_result]
theorem        = PAR-011 Fixed-Point Commutativity
statement      = If F: ℂⁿ → ℂⁿ has constant nonzero Jacobian determinant, then the
                 fixed point ρ* of the Jordan operator T satisfies [U, ρ*] = 0,
                 forcing ρ* ∈ Comm(U) = polynomial algebra. Therefore F⁻¹ is polynomial.
